WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.583
SAAD ALI: All right,
name is Saad Ali.

00:00:01.583 --> 00:00:03.010
I'm a software
engineer at Google,

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:04.240
here at KubeCon Seattle.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.130
And now, I'm here
with Tim Hockin,

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.170
the granddaddy of Kubernetes.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.680
He does not need
an introduction.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.346
But for those of you
who don't know him,

00:00:11.346 --> 00:00:12.820
he's been involved
with the project

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.020
from the very beginning.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:18.582
And he's helped architect a
lot of the Kubernetes project.

00:00:18.582 --> 00:00:21.040
So Tim, can you tell us a little
bit about those early days

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.560
and how the project
has evolved since then?

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.540
TIM HOCKIN: Sure.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.020
The early days was really about
bringing out some of the ideas

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.244
that we had in Borg,
taking some of the lessons

00:00:31.244 --> 00:00:32.619
that we had learned
through Borg,

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:35.050
and bringing them out
to rest of the world.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.995
We saw the Docker
thing happening.

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:38.120
And it was pretty exciting.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.760
But we knew where
that road would go.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.110
And we knew that something
like Borg would be needed.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.950
So we wanted to take
the best lessons

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.560
that we had learned through
the Borg and Omega process

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.810
and share them with the world.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.180
It was a lot of fun
to be able to do that.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:58.320
And from then on, the community
has grown at this amazing pace.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.500
We're here, standing among
8,000 of my best friends.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.124
And it's a little overwhelming.

00:01:04.124 --> 00:01:05.290
SAAD ALI: That it's awesome.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.240
So what are some
of the challenges

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.620
that the community
is facing now?

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.860
As you said, it's grown
to a tremendous scale.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.500
There are more
contributors now than ever.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.300
How are we dealing with that?

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.790
And what are some of the
challenges that come with it?

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.498
TIM HOCKIN: We got a
bunch of challenges.

00:01:20.498 --> 00:01:22.760
Some of them are, how
do we stay on track?

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.571
How do we not lose focus of the
core principles of the project?

00:01:25.571 --> 00:01:27.820
We need to make sure that
we don't try to do too much.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.530
We stay within our swim lane.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.885
But we want to be sure to
empower other communities--

00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:34.510
other projects.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.870
Projects like Istio and Knative
are really interesting to me,

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.240
because they build
on top of Kubernetes.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.550
You can see more and
more effort in Kubernetes

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.970
going into extensibility
and mechanisms around that.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:49.670
There's a bunch of talks here
at KubeCon about extensibility.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.960
So we're really leveraging that
to make sure the project stays

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.740
at a reasonable size.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.560
The community
contributors, now, are

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.310
dealing with issues of
compatibility and growth

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:01.780
and scope.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.240
Every new change has a chance to
break every one of these users.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.720
And so we have to be more
careful than we've ever been.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.130
SAAD ALI: And so, one of your
talks this week was about CRDs.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.530
And that's one of the main
mechanisms for extensibility.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.710
Can we deep dive into that a
little bit and get technical?

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.890
What are some of the
challenges with CRDs?

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:21.880
Why do they exist?

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.320
How should people use them?

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.020
Are they only for
external people?

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.899
Are they part of
the core project?

00:02:27.899 --> 00:02:28.690
How does that work?

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.460
TIM HOCKIN: Thanks
for the lead-in.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.017
This was my lightning
talk this week.

00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:34.600
It was really about
the birth of CRDs,

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.150
Custom Resource
Definitions-- this is one

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.340
of our extensibility
mechanisms--

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.572
and how that's being
used in Kubernetes.

00:02:41.572 --> 00:02:43.030
And it was one of
those things that

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.084
was surprising and emergent.

00:02:45.084 --> 00:02:47.500
It's one of these things, if
you set out to do on purpose,

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.994
you probably would fail at.

00:02:48.994 --> 00:02:51.160
But because it emerged
organically from the project,

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.922
it was really successful.

00:02:52.922 --> 00:02:54.880
It's a way that people
are extending Kubernetes

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.671
to add their own types,
their own concepts,

00:02:56.671 --> 00:02:58.690
into our API machinery.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.377
It started off as a thing
for people to just prototype

00:03:01.377 --> 00:03:01.960
and play with.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.350
And it wasn't really very good.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.250
It was very second-class.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.991
But now, it's very much
a first-class thing.

00:03:07.991 --> 00:03:10.240
We've been adding more and
more support for versioning

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.704
and schema and
validation, to the point

00:03:12.704 --> 00:03:14.870
now that you can actually
build real things with it.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.350
And I know you know
this, because we made you

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:19.414
and your SIG be one of
our pioneers, right?

00:03:19.414 --> 00:03:20.080
SAAD ALI: Right.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.621
TIM HOCKIN: And you guys, thanks
for falling on that grenade.

00:03:22.621 --> 00:03:25.880
But like, some of the storage
work is happening as CRDs.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:27.550
It is not just an extension.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:29.440
It's actually built-in
as part of the system.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.840
It's just a resource
definition that's not in code.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:32.980
It's in declaration.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.313
SAAD ALI: So what does that
mean for the built-in types?

00:03:35.313 --> 00:03:37.700
Are there going to be
no more built-in types?

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.290
What does the future look like?

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:41.080
TIM HOCKIN: Well,
long-term, I would

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:44.710
like to see us with an empty API
server that's got nothing in it

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:48.250
except namespaces, custom
resource definitions, admission

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:51.790
control web hooks, and all the
stuff you need to run CRDs.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:53.980
Everything else gets kicked
out-- pods, services,

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:56.380
deployments-- all get moved
out into custom resource

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:57.730
definitions.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.570
And Kubernetes machinery
becomes a separate project.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.490
And it's a thing that
you load type sets into.

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:04.784
And you go forward from there.

00:04:04.784 --> 00:04:06.700
SAAD ALI: And so this
Kubernetes API Machinery

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.720
can then be utilized
by multiple projects.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.690
It's not going to be
Kubernetes specific.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:13.250
It could be used by Istio
and other projects as well.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.511
TIM HOCKIN: Absolutely,
this is already happening.

00:04:15.511 --> 00:04:18.010
But today, we're managing it
through several different forks

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:19.310
of the code base.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:21.670
So we have a generic API
server and an example extension

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:22.402
server.

00:04:22.402 --> 00:04:24.110
And we shouldn't need
to do those things.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.170
They should all be
on the same basis.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:27.280
SAAD ALI: Got it.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:28.630
Let's talk about the
community a little bit.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.629
The community, of course,
has grown tremendously

00:04:30.629 --> 00:04:31.990
in the last few years.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.320
How do we manage that?

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.910
What are some of the challenges
with a community of that scale?

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:40.330
And I know, you served on the
Steering Committee for a while.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.850
Can you tell us what the
Steering Committee's view

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.716
on this is?

00:04:44.716 --> 00:04:46.090
TIM HOCKIN: The
community is what

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.500
makes this project successful.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:51.530
Unambiguously, that is what
makes it unique in the world.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:55.100
And we need to make sure that
we make that healthy for people.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.690
We have tens of thousands of
contributors at this point.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:00.954
And honestly, the weak
spots that we have, I think,

00:05:00.954 --> 00:05:02.620
are the same as a lot
of other projects.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.627
It's things like
documentation and onboarding.

00:05:06.627 --> 00:05:08.710
We're never going to be
quite good enough on that.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:10.060
But we're spending a
lot of time and energy

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.210
trying to make
those things better.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.930
We've got a whole SIG that is
just devoted to the contributor

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:15.720
experience and
making things better.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.470
We had our Contributors'
Summit yesterday,

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.469
where we had
400-ish contributors

00:05:20.469 --> 00:05:21.760
and would-be contributors here.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.020
SAAD ALI: 500 on the wait list.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.853
TIM HOCKIN: Yeah, and
more on the wait list.

00:05:24.853 --> 00:05:27.340
They were here to talk
about how to contribute

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:30.790
to Kubernetes, what the
state of the project is.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:33.220
So I think we need to make
sure that we are doing things

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:34.630
to make these people successful.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:37.540
They are the way that
we are able to grow

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:40.525
at the rocket-ship growth
that we've been having.

00:05:40.525 --> 00:05:42.400
SAAD ALI: And what are
some of those projects

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.774
that we're doing
to help continue

00:05:43.774 --> 00:05:45.504
to sustain that growth?

00:05:45.504 --> 00:05:47.920
TIM HOCKIN: We've got everything
from better documentation

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.930
and onboarding-- we had
a New Contributor track

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:51.013
at the Contributor Summit.

00:05:51.013 --> 00:05:53.050
So this is for people
who are just starting out

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:54.466
with Kubernetes
to come and learn,

00:05:54.466 --> 00:05:56.170
how does our code base work?

00:05:56.170 --> 00:05:56.950
How do our bots--

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:59.020
all of the GitHub workflow
that we've built--

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.010
how do those things work?

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:03.100
We've also got people who
are building those bots.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:06.312
We've got a group of people
we call EngProd at Google.

00:06:06.312 --> 00:06:08.020
They're building these
things like robots

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.190
that make interacting with
GitHub a little smoother,

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.200
a little easier--

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.229
and scaling up a
little bit better.

00:06:13.229 --> 00:06:15.520
Just among things, we've also
got all the documentation

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:18.490
people, which is serving
purposes both for end-users

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.680
and for new contributors.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:23.260
SAAD ALI: In terms of leadership
in the community, what I've

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:25.660
noticed, as I talked to Ash
and Janet and other folks,

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:29.980
is that this community
really incubates new leaders.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:30.730
Is that natural?

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:32.229
Is that something
that was intended?

00:06:32.229 --> 00:06:33.820
And how does that happen?

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.500
And what are your
thoughts on that?

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.020
TIM HOCKIN: I take it as a
real compliment actually.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.560
It's very intentional.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.160
We know that projects
only survive as long

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:43.450
as their leadership survives.

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:45.506
And I know that I
won't be here forever.

00:06:45.506 --> 00:06:47.380
I want this project to
be around a long time.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:48.460
So I want to make
sure that there's

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.030
people who know
what they're doing,

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:52.930
who have some real battle scars,
who know where the bodies are

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:55.240
buried, who are coming
up through the ranks

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.466
to take over the project.

00:06:56.466 --> 00:06:58.090
I think this is a
really important part

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:00.500
of the way the Steering
Committee is set up.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.290
We set it up in a way
that, all the people

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:03.640
who originated the project--

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.950
only a few of us can possibly
be on Steering Committee

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:07.340
after the end of this year.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.100
And I think it's
really important,

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.320
so that we've got
fresh blood coming in,

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:13.630
fresh eyes, fresh perspectives.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:14.664
SAAD ALI: Awesome.

00:07:14.664 --> 00:07:17.080
So let's switch gears a little
bit and talk about KubeCon.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.282
This is probably your--
what?-- sixth KubeCon?

00:07:19.282 --> 00:07:20.490
TIM HOCKIN: Worldwide-- yeah.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:24.040
SAAD ALI: Yeah, so can you talk
about what changes you've seen

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.050
and what you're looking
forward to this week?

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:27.508
TIM HOCKIN: Well,
the first KubeCon

00:07:27.508 --> 00:07:29.620
was 400 and something people.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:33.110
So we're roughly 20 times
bigger than that this time,

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:35.260
so it's a little bit
of a different thing.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:38.230
But at the same time, I walk
around the show floor here,

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.720
and I'm constantly seeing
people that I know-- people

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:42.760
I've worked with on the
project, ex-Googlers,

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:46.480
people I worked with at other
companies, friends of friends.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:47.560
It's amazing.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.495
It really is like hanging out
with 8,000 of my best buddies.

00:07:50.495 --> 00:07:52.870
Went to the party last night--
there was a reception last

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:53.530
night--

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.670
and just hung out with
people and just talked.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:56.890
And sometimes, we
talked about Kubernetes.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:58.750
And sometimes, we talked
about other stuff.

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:01.220
And it's really awesome.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:03.040
It's a little
overwhelming, honestly.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.940
The keynote room
is pretty massive.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:09.190
But it's amazing that
these people are all here

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:11.140
to talk about this
thing that is making,

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:13.400
hopefully, their lives
a little bit better.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:14.830
SAAD ALI: Yeah, that's awesome.

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:16.520
Last question-- put
you on the spot.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:18.460
What is your favorite
KubeCon memory?

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:21.010
TIM HOCKIN: My favorite
KubeCon memory--

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:28.360
boy-- probably the first
KubeCon at this hotel

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:35.149
in San Francisco, where it was
amazing to see 400 people here

00:08:35.149 --> 00:08:36.190
to talk about this thing.

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:37.630
And they were all
early adopters,

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:39.697
the people who had actually
been using the system

00:08:39.697 --> 00:08:40.530
and playing with it.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:42.429
And I knew almost everybody.

00:08:42.429 --> 00:08:46.420
And Brian, Clayton, and
I sequestered ourselves

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:50.230
in a back room and put up on
the whiteboard out in the lobby,

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:53.581
come to room whatever and get
your PRs shot down in person.

00:08:53.581 --> 00:08:55.330
And we had a line of
people who were there

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.230
to get their PRs live-reviewed.

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:59.620
SAAD ALI: Nice, we
can try that again

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:01.760
and see how long that line is.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:03.260
All right, thank
you very much, Tim.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:03.490
TIM HOCKIN: Thanks, Saad.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:04.780
SAAD ALI: It was a
pleasure talking to you.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:05.390
Take care.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:06.223
TIM HOCKIN: You too.

00:09:06.223 --> 00:09:08.200
[MUSICAL JINGLE]

